1.CHAR () :
 ❖	It is used to store characters such as ‘A-Z’, ‘a-z’, ‘0-9’, alphanumeric values, special characters (!, @, #, $, %, ^, &, *, etc.).
 ❖	Always we must enclose the characters in Single Quotes (‘ ’).
 ❖	Whenever we write CHAR datatype we must include Size to it.
 ❖	The maximum number of characters can be stored is up to 2000 characters.
 ❖	Char follows fixed length memory allocation.
     NOTE:  Recommended to use for fixed-size values. 
     EX: Pancard_no, Aadhar_number, pincode, Country_code, State_Abbrevation

2. VARCHAR ():
 ❖	It is used to store characters such as ‘A-Z’, ‘a-z’, ‘0-9’, alphanumeric values, special characters (!, @, #, $, %, ^, &, *, etc.).
 ❖	Always we must enclose the characters in Single Quotes (‘ ’).
 ❖	Whenever we write VARCHAR datatype we must include Size to it.
 ❖	The maximum number of characters can be stored is up to 2000 characters.
 ❖	Varchar follows variable-length memory allocation.

3. VARCHAR2() :
 ❖	It is the Updated Version of Varchar.
 ❖	Maximum number of characters can be stored is up to 4000 characters.
 ❖	If you assign varchar data type to any column by default SQL engine will assign it is as Varchar2.
    NOTE:
      If you are assigning a varchar data type to the column. By default, the SQL engine will take it as VA RCHAR2.

