3. DATE:
 ❖	It is used to store the date in a particular format.
 ❖	It is mandatory to enter date in Single quotes.
   We have 2 specified formats:
   ORACLE SPECIFIED FORMAT:
     1.‘DD-MON-YYYY’
     2.‘DD-MON-YY’

NOTE: 
   1.	What happens if we insert date data in below format: ex: ‘31-DEC-00’ How my compiler will interpret it whether it consider the year as 2000 or it can 1800,1900 etc..
   2.	In general when we assign the “DATE” data type by default it accepts date month, year along with time of the day (i.e) hours, minute, second.
   3.	But remember: even though it displays only date, the time is still stored in the background.
   4.	We can store from January 1, 4712 BC up to December 31, 9999 AD.
   5.	Oracle uses an internal 7-byte storage format to keep date + time.
   6.	When the data inserted is ‘31-NOV-00’, Oracle uses a pivot year rule when you enter a 2-digit year :‘DD-MON-RR’ .
        By default: 00 to 49 → Treated as 2000 to 2049
                    50 to 99 → Treated as 1950 to 1999
