4. NUMBER:
 ⮚	It is used to store the numeric values. 
     Syntax : Number(Precision[Scale]);
PRECISION:  
 ⮚	It represents integer values. 
 ⮚	It is Mandatory. 
 ⮚	Its range varies from 1 to 38. 
 ⮚	No default value. 
SCALE: 
 ⮚	It represents decimal values. 
 ⮚	It is Optional. 
 ⮚	Its range varies from -84 to 127. 
 ⮚	The default value of scale is 0.


NOTE:
What happens if give scale in negative since it ranges from -84 to 127?
Step-1 Syntax: Number (p, s):
❖	Here compiler checks for scale (since scale is negative)
❖	Example: NUMBER (F7, -1) 
❖	Since here scale value is -1 it looks for one’s place value for round off 
❖	(if it is -2 ten’s place, -3 means hundred’s place, -4 means thousand’s place)
❖	In negative scale values it’ll not look for placement of decimal point instead it’ll try to round off.
    1.Number (7 ,-1) –  inserted value is 1234567 
   Since scale is -1 it’ll look for one’s digit (i.e) 7 and that 7>5 so compiler will round up so the value it’ll store is 1234570. Still the precision length is 7 only so it’ll be accepted by compiler.
    2.Number (7, -1) – inserted value is 1234563 
   Since scale is -1 it’ll look for one’s digit (i.e) 3 and that 7<5 so compiler will round down so the value it’ll store is 1234560. Still the precision length is 7 only so it’ll be accepted by compiler


ADDITIONAL DATA TYPES:
Character Data types:
NCHAR () & NVARCHAR ()
❖	Both NCHAR () & NVARCHAR () are used to store Unicode character let’s say multilingual data like Hindi, Tamil, Telugu etc.…
❖	If it is fixed length we will go for NCHAR ().
❖	If it is variable length we will go for NVARCHAR ().
             SYNTAX: NCHAR(SIZE) & NVARCHAR(SIZE)

