2. TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE:
Stores date + time + time zone (date + time + fractional seconds + exact time zone).
            SYNTAX: TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE
Real-world use case:
❖	Flight booking systems (departure at 2025-09-04 10:00 AM +05:30, arrival at 2025-09-04 08:00 AM +03:00).
❖	Financial transactions (banks need exact TZ to avoid disputes).

3. TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE :
Stores date/time normalized to the database time zone, but shows it in user’s time zone.
     SYNTAX: TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE
Real-world use case:
This datatype is very useful for applications with global users (e.g., Gmail, Zoom meetings, stock trading).

4. INTERVAL YEAR TO MONTH :
Stores a period/duration in years and months (No Days, Hours, Minutes, Seconds).
     SYNTAX: INTERVAL YEAR TO MONTH

Ex: "2 years 3 months".
 
Real-world use case:
❖	Storing subscription periods (2 years 6 months).
❖	Employee experience duration.
❖	Loan tenures.

5. INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND :
Stores a period/Duration in days, hours, minutes, seconds.
Ex: "5 days 04:30:15".
        SYNTAX: INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND
Real-world use case:
Delivery tracking (package took 3 days 5 hours 20 mins 15 secs).
Measuring execution time of programs.
Sports timing (e.g., Olympics: 0 days 0:00:09.876).

NUMERIC DATA TYPES:
INTEGER/INT :
Oracle treats INTEGER or INT as NUMBER (38).
Same as NUMBER (38), means you can store big whole numbers (up to 38 digits).
 It is not a separate type, just a subtype of NUMBER.
       SYNTAX: INTEGER
      Ex:10, 999, 123456789

2. FLOAT :
❖	It is used to store an approximate floating point number (decimal number (but not exact)).
❖	It’s an approximate value → sometimes the last digits may change a little.
❖	Default precision = 126 binary digits (≈ 38 decimal digits).
              SYNTAX: FLOAT[(binary_precision)]
   Ex: You type 123.4567, it may store as 123.457
